# React and Next.js – PandaTrack

## Server Components by default

In Next.js App Router use Server Components by default. Prefer them whenever possible; add `"use client"` only when needed (hooks, events, state, browser APIs).

When a part of the tree must be interactive, keep the client boundary minimal but cohesive: split so only that part is a Client Component, but group by **logical unit** (e.g. one form, one menu, one accordion). Avoid both making the whole page a Client Component and fragmenting into many tiny Client Components. Do not use "client" or "server" in file names; the boundary is identified by the `"use client"` directive in the code.

Reuse existing components from `src/components/core/` and `src/components/modules/` before introducing new Client Components. For example, use the core `Button` for interactive buttons instead of creating a new Client Component per button. For native fields, use core **`Input`**, **`Textarea`**, and **`Select`** instead of inlining `<input>`, `<textarea>`, or `<select>` with duplicated Tailwind stacks. Create a new Client Component only when (1) no core or module component fits the need, or (2) the interactivity is a full logical block (form, menu, carousel, etc.) that deserves its own component.

Before creating a route-level component, also check whether the same pattern already exists in the current route subtree's parent `_components/share/` folder. If the same interactive pattern is now needed by a second page in the same subtree, promote it there in the same change instead of creating another page-local copy.

Avoid "copy, rename, tweak" component creation. If the new component starts from an existing file with only minor differences in labels, options, layout slots, or handlers, extract a shared component instead of maintaining parallel copies.

## Small, focused components

Keep components small with a single clear responsibility. If business logic grows, extract hooks or utilities.

## Constants outside the component

Declare constants and variables that do not depend on props or state outside the component.

```typescript
const DEFAULT_AVATAR_SIZE = 40;

const UserAvatar = ({ user, size = DEFAULT_AVATAR_SIZE }) => {
  // ...
};
```

## Props order (recommended)

Suggested order: native HTML attributes first, then custom props and handlers.

```tsx
<Button
  type="submit"
  className="primary-button"
  disabled={isLoading}
  customProp={value}
  onClick={handleClick}
>
  Submit
</Button>
```

## Event handler logic

Extract logic into named functions instead of inline in the handler.

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid
<button onClick={() => { setLoading(true); fetchData(); }}>Click</button>

// ✅ Do this
const handleClick = () => {
  setLoading(true);
  fetchData();
};
<button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>
```

## Prop drilling limit (3 levels)

If you need to pass props more than 3 levels deep, use React Context instead of continuing to pass props.

## Composition over many props

Prefer composition with subcomponents or slots over components with many props.

```tsx
// ✅ Composition approach
<UserCard user={user}>
  <UserCard.Avatar />
  <UserCard.Info>
    <UserCard.Name />
    <UserCard.Email />
  </UserCard.Info>
  <UserCard.Actions>
    <EditButton onClick={handleEdit} />
  </UserCard.Actions>
</UserCard>
```

## Memoization

Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` only for expensive computations or functions passed to many children; not for trivial values or functions.

## Loading UI, skeletons, and `next/dynamic` (App Router)

**Problem to avoid:** Adding skeletons or `dynamic(..., { loading })` for Client Components that are still **server-rendered** (`ssr: true`, the default) inside a Server Component page. In that setup, the HTML and RSC payload already include the real markup; users wait on the **full server response**, not on a client chunk that would show a fallback. The `loading` UI then **almost never appears**, but the code adds complexity and maintenance cost for no visible benefit.

**Do not** introduce route-level skeletons or dynamic `loading` fallbacks for that pattern unless there is a **measured** bundle problem and an explicit, reviewed tradeoff (document briefly in PR or `docs/` if non-obvious).

**When loading or skeleton UI does make sense**

- **`loading.tsx`** in a route segment: while Next is still resolving the **server** work for that segment (slow DB, slow RSC). This is server-pending UI, not JavaScript chunk loading.
- **`Suspense`** with a fallback around **async Server Components** or streaming server data where the framework can show the fallback until the server sends that subtree.
- **Inside a Client Component**: explicit states the user triggers (e.g. submit pending, lazy sub-panel opened, `ssr: false` island where product accepts empty-first paint). Not as a default for every heavy form on an SSR page.
- **`next/dynamic` with `ssr: false`**: only when the product accepts client-only rendering for that subtree; understand SEO and first-paint implications. Do not pair `ssr: false` with server-driven critical forms unless that is intentional.

**Skills and external guides** (e.g. Vercel React/Next performance docs) still apply for waterfalls, memoization, and bundle size, but they **must not override** this rule: do not add skeletons or dynamic `loading` props that imply a client loading phase when the UI is actually delivered by the server in the same navigation.

For performance and advanced React/Next patterns (waterfalls, Suspense, RSC), follow the guides in `.agents/skills/vercel-react-best-practices/AGENTS.md` when they apply, **in addition to** the constraints above.
